WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:01.199
Module seven:

2
00:00:01.199 --> 00:00:02.570
fundamental analysis.

3
00:00:02.570 --> 00:00:04.855
What stands behind a token's price:

4
00:00:04.855 --> 00:00:08.968
the path from revenue to the holder, the issuance of new coins,

5
00:00:08.968 --> 00:00:14.126
the link between network activity and price, and the traps of on-chain metrics.

6
00:00:15.126 --> 00:00:18.536
A share has the company's profit and a right to part of it.

7
00:00:18.536 --> 00:00:20.675
A token has no such right by default.

8
00:00:20.675 --> 00:00:24.085
A protocol can collect hundreds of millions in fees a year,

9
00:00:24.085 --> 00:00:27.958
but if liquidity providers get them, the token holder gets nothing.

10
00:00:27.958 --> 00:00:31.542
That is why people look at the path from revenue to the token.

11
00:00:32.542 --> 00:00:33.944
There are three paths.

12
00:00:33.944 --> 00:00:34.773
Distribution:

13
00:00:34.773 --> 00:00:38.215
part of the fees is paid to those who stake the token.

14
00:00:38.215 --> 00:00:39.298
Buyback and burn:

15
00:00:39.298 --> 00:00:42.931
the protocol buys tokens on the market and destroys them.

16
00:00:42.931 --> 00:00:46.118
And voting rights, which are a promise, not money.

17
00:00:46.118 --> 00:00:50.389
When there is a path, you can calculate a multiple, as with stocks:

18
00:00:50.389 --> 00:00:55.105
for mature companies it is 10 to 30, while for many tokens it is hundreds,

19
00:00:55.105 --> 00:00:55.870
or infinity.

20
00:00:56.870 --> 00:01:00.304
People talk a lot about demand and less about supply,

21
00:01:00.304 --> 00:01:02.571
although supply is easier to count.

22
00:01:02.571 --> 00:01:07.754
Issuance happens every day, unlocks release batches of team and investor tokens,

23
00:01:07.754 --> 00:01:09.697
and the recipients often sell.

24
00:01:09.697 --> 00:01:13.909
If a coin issues eight percent a year and the price stands still,

25
00:01:13.909 --> 00:01:16.630
demand has in fact grown by eight percent:

26
00:01:16.630 --> 00:01:17.990
the new coins ate it.

27
00:01:18.990 --> 00:01:19.971
For comparison:

28
00:01:19.971 --> 00:01:25.135
bitcoin's issuance is now under one percent a year and halves every four years.

29
00:01:25.135 --> 00:01:30.429
Young tokens often have a fully diluted value five or even ten times their market

30
00:01:30.429 --> 00:01:30.691
cap.

31
00:01:30.691 --> 00:01:33.894
Ahead of them are several years of large unlocks.

32
00:01:34.894 --> 00:01:38.813
If a network is used more and more, should the coin get dearer?

33
00:01:38.813 --> 00:01:41.053
Over long stretches there is a link,

34
00:01:41.053 --> 00:01:44.599
but on a horizon of months it drowns in market sentiment.

35
00:01:44.599 --> 00:01:48.829
In 2022 the bitcoin network worked flawlessly and the hashrate grew,

36
00:01:48.829 --> 00:01:51.753
yet the price fell by more than three quarters.

37
00:01:51.753 --> 00:01:55.174
Rates, liquidity and the appetite for risk had changed.

38
00:01:56.174 --> 00:01:59.551
A blockchain is open, and a lot can be counted:

39
00:01:59.551 --> 00:02:03.071
transfers, addresses, fees, inflows to exchanges.

40
00:02:03.071 --> 00:02:05.083
But openness is not honesty.

41
00:02:05.083 --> 00:02:09.322
Address counts are inflated with millions of empty wallets,

42
00:02:09.322 --> 00:02:13.058
volume by sending the same coins around in a circle.

43
00:02:13.058 --> 00:02:16.506
What is harder to fake is what costs real money:

44
00:02:16.506 --> 00:02:18.518
fees, revenue, burned coins.

45
00:02:19.518 --> 00:02:20.375
The conclusion.

46
00:02:20.375 --> 00:02:25.117
Fundamental data answers the question of what this is and what it might be worth in

47
00:02:25.117 --> 00:02:25.459
years.

48
00:02:25.459 --> 00:02:28.944
But it is poorly suited to deciding whether to buy this week.

49
00:02:28.944 --> 00:02:32.886
And the numbers worth trusting are those someone pays real money for.

50
00:02:33.886 --> 00:02:34.842
The main points.

51
00:02:34.842 --> 00:02:39.620
What matters is whether there is a path from the protocol's revenue to the token

52
00:02:39.620 --> 00:02:40.039
holder.

53
00:02:40.039 --> 00:02:42.547
Issuance and unlocks are constant sellers.

54
00:02:42.547 --> 00:02:47.326
And network activity and the coin's price are weakly linked and with a long lag.
